// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-080
//
// PROBE 16b — A CONSUMER CANNOT EVEN READ `Vec<T>.items`. The hidden bare
// pointer, asserted by a refusal.
//
// ASKS: may code outside `src/core/vec.npk` index a `Vec`'s `items` directly?
//
// MATTERS BECAUSE `items` is a `wild T->` — a BARE POINTER, which the language
// does not bounds-check (TM-108, `SAFETY.md` S-17b): `probe13d` measured an
// out-of-range read through one returning a planted value at exit 0. Until
// cycle 0.1.0c the only thing keeping every index inside the guarded accessors
// was `check_raw_index`, a tree check over `src/` that no consumer ever meets.
//
// ANSWER, measured at `c3bdae2` (cycle 0.1.0c, TM-156): NO. `items` is
// `hidden` (the compiler's D-314): neither read nor written outside the
// declaring module, so `v.items[0i64]` below is `NITPICK-TYPE-080`, exactly
// once — and the body would otherwise run correctly, since index 0 is the
// element just pushed, so the refusal is about ACCESS and not about the
// index. THE REFUSAL IS THE ASSERTION (B-7). `probe16e` is the positive twin
// for the fields that stay readable.
mod:probe16b_vec_items_read_refused;

use "../../src/core/vec.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    Vec<int64>:v = raw vec_init::<int64>(4i64);
    drop vec_push(@v, 7i64);
    int64:x = v.items[0i64];
    drop vec_free(@v);
    if (x != 7i64) { exit 3i32; }
    exit 0i32;
};

// A SUPERSET of what `NITPICK-REACH-002` asks, deliberately (`SAFETY.md` S-6b,
// constraint 3: a superset compiles). If a later compiler reordered its phases
// so that the reach analysis ran before the check this file asserts, a
// failsafe naming exactly the demanded arms could add a stray `REACH-002` to
// the refusal; this one cannot.
func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest)    { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)           { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)       { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)       { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)       { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)          { exit 96i32; },
        (DivByZero)         { exit 97i32; },
        (DivOverflow)       { exit 98i32; },
        (StackExhausted)    { exit 106i32; },
        (MachineFault)      { exit 107i32; },
        (DecreasesViolated) { exit 108i32; },
        (LimitViolated)     { exit 109i32; },
        (*)                 { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
